/* book: the chapter, paragraph by paragraph (first sentences):
   - Take a single-cell tube under a pure torque T, with no axial load.
   - Now take moments about any point O.
   - q is the shear flow (N/mm); T is the torque (N·mm); A is the area enclosed by the wall mid-line (mm²); t is the local wall thickness (mm); τ is the shear stress (N/mm²), largest where the wall is thinnest.
   - Use the area inside the mid-line, not the outer outline.
   - The section rotates by θ about its centre of twist.
   - dθ/dz is the rate of twist (rad/mm); G is the shear modulus (N/mm²); ∮ds/t is dimensionless (a length over a length) and is summed wall by wall when t changes.
   - J is the torsion constant of the closed section (mm⁴); L is the length of the member (mm); θ is the total twist (rad).
   - For a circular tube of mean radius R and thickness t, A = πR² and ∮ds/t = 2πR/t, so J = 2πR³t, the polar second moment of a thin ring.
   - The result becomes inaccurate rather than failing outright as the wall thickens, because the stress is no longer uniform through t.
*/
